s of clinical stability.\n\nAges Eligible for Study\n\n18 Years and older (Adult,  Older Adult )\n\nSexes Eligible for Study\n\nAll\n\nAccepts Healthy Volunteers\n\nNo\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStudy Details | NCT06346977 | Diagnosis of Leptomeningeal Metastasis and the Monitoring of Intrathecal Chemotherapy Efficacy in NSCLC | ClinicalTrials.gov (https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT06346977?aggFilters=status%3Anot+rec+ava&cond=Diagnosis&rank=5&viewType=Table)\nciteturn2search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.6 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; ClinicalTrials.gov ID NCT06346977 ... Non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), occupying a disquieting position as the second most prevalent and deadliest neoplasm worldwide, afflicts an estimated 30% of its patients with intracranial metastatic spread. ... A Prospective Study on the Diagnosis of Leptomeningeal Metastasis and the Monitoring of Intrathecal Chemotherapy Efficacy in NSCLC Based on Proteomics\n\n## Diagnosis of Leptomeningeal Metastasis and the Monitoring of Intrathecal Chemotherapy Efficacy in NSCLC\n\nClinicalTrials.gov ID NCT06346977\n\nSponsor Union Hospital, Tongji Medical College, Huazhong University of Science and Technology\n\nInformation provided by Xiaorong Dong, Union Hospital, Tongji Medical College, Huazhong University of Science and Technology (Responsible Party)\n\nLast Update Posted 2024-04-04\n\nUnknown status \n\nLast verified\n\n2024-04 by Xiaorong Dong, Union Hospital, Tongji Medical College, Huazhong University of Science and Technology\n\nLast known status was\n\nNot yet recruiting\n\n## Study Overview\n\nBrief Summary\n\nNon-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), occupying a disquieting position as the second most prevalent and deadliest neoplasm worldwide, afflicts an estimated 30% of its patients with intracranial metastatic spread. Among these, leptomeningeal metastasis (LM) is an exceptionally surreptitious and perilous manifestation, often evading timely and accurate diagnosis. The clinical landscape is further complicated by the presence of patients who, due to various reasons, are unable to undergo lumbar puncture, a procedure crucial for the investigation of LM. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStudy Details | NCT04941430 | 7T MRI Scan for the Early Detection of Melanoma Brain Metastases | ClinicalTrials.gov (https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT04941430)\nciteturn2search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; ClinicalTrials.gov ID NCT04941430 ... Evaluate incidence of diagnosis of leptomeningeal disease on CE 7T MRI brain. ... Pilot Study of 7T MRI for Early Diagnosis of Melanoma Brain Metastases ... Clinical Stage IV Cutaneous Melanoma AJCC v8 Metastatic Malignant Neoplasm in the Brain Metastatic Melanoma Pathologic Stage IV Cutaneous Melanoma AJCC v8\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStudy Details | NCT03020 | LMB-7 Immunotoxin in Treating Patients With Leptomeningeal Metastases | ClinicalTrials.gov (https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT03020)\nciteturn2search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.7 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; To learn more, please see the Contacts and Locations section in How to Read a Study Record(https://clinicaltrials.gov/study-basics/how-to-read-study-record#contacts-and-locations). ... For general information about clinical research, read Learn About Studies(https://clinicaltrials.gov/study-basics/learn-about-studies). ... DISEASE CHARACTERISTICS: Histologically proven primary intracranial or extraneural neoplasm with leptomeningeal metastases At least 30% of malignant cells in the cerebrospinal fluid, primary tumor, or metastatic tumor must react with the B3 mouse monoclonal antibody\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStudy Results | NCT04460937 | Testing the Addition of an Anti-cancer Drug, Adavosertib, to Radiation Therapy for 